/* ==========================================================================
   FORM

   A page of labelled rows, and the footer that acts on them. The row owns the
   alignment; the control inside it only says how wide it is.
   ========================================================================== */

/* ==========================================================================
   PAGE
   ========================================================================== */

/* A page is a scroll view now, so it has to be able to give way as well as grow. A flex
   item will not shrink below the height of what is inside it unless it is told to, and
   without that the scroll view would stand at the full height of its rows, push the footer
   out through the bottom of the panel, and never scroll - the content would just be cut. */

.page {
    flex-grow: 1;
    flex-shrink: 1;

    min-height: 0;
}


/* Room for the scrollbar to sit beside the rows rather than over the value at the end of
   them. The same width the lobby's roster reserves. */

.page .unity-scroll-view__content-container {
    padding-right: var(--space-s);
}


/* ==========================================================================
   FORM FIELD
   ========================================================================== */

.field {
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;

    margin-top: var(--space-xs);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-xs);
}


.field__label {
    width: var(--label-width);

    /* The gap between a setting's name and the setting, taken out of the
       label's own column rather than added to the control's side.
       The column's edge is a line other things are lined up against - the hint
       under a control in the lobby is pushed out by exactly --label-width - so
       moving the control would have moved it away from the words explaining
       it. Padding leaves the edge where it is and steps the text back from it. */
    padding-right: var(--space-m);

    flex-shrink: 0;

    align-self: stretch;

    color: var(--text);
    font-size: var(--font-size-s);

    /* Wraps, because the column it sits in is a fixed width and a label that
       does not wrap does not stay in it - it runs on across the control it is
       naming. Two lines of label is a row that got taller; a sentence written
       over a dropdown is a screen that looks broken.

       Russian is wider than English at the same size and the text setting adds
       a third on top of that, so this is not an edge case: half the rows on
       the settings screen were doing it. */
    white-space: normal;

    -unity-text-align: middle-left;
}


/* Every control in a row starts where the label column ends, and so does the
   line of text under it. That was three pixels short of true.

   Unity's own theme puts a small margin down both sides of every control it
   ships, and a Label has none - so a dropdown, a toggle and a slider all sat a
   few pixels right of the hint explaining them. Small enough to look like
   nothing and exactly the size that makes a form look untidy without saying
   why.

   The vertical half of that margin was already being turned off here. The
   horizontal half was being turned off further in, one inner element at a time
   - the dropdown's box, the toggle's checkmark - which fixed what those parts
   looked like and never reached the control they hang off. This is where every
   control in a row passes through, so this is where it goes. */

.field__control {
    width: var(--control-width);

    flex-shrink: 0;

    align-items: center;

    margin-top: 0;
    margin-bottom: 0;
    margin-left: 0;
    margin-right: 0;

    color: var(--text);
}


.field__value {
    color: var(--text-dim);
    font-size: var(--font-size-s);

    margin-left: var(--space-s);

    align-self: stretch;

    -unity-text-align: middle-left;
}


/* ==========================================================================
   FOOTER
   ========================================================================== */

.footer {
    flex-direction: row;
    justify-content: flex-end;

    /* A hairline above the actions, so a panel has a floor rather than just
       running out. Cheap detail, and it does the same work as whitespace at a
       quarter of the height. */
    border-top-width: var(--border);
    border-top-color: var(--line);

    padding-top: var(--space-m);
    margin-top: var(--space-l);
}


/* What a button looks like in a row of actions, said here rather than in
   Button.uss: the button carries what it means, the place carries how it sits.
   It was split across both files, and a selector whose declarations live in two
   places agrees with itself only for as long as nobody reorders the imports.

   The width arrived to stop a hover from shoving the neighbouring word
   sideways, back when hovering changed how wide a word was. Nothing moves any
   more, so it is not holding anything up - it stays because a row of actions
   all the same width is what a row of actions should look like, which was true
   before there was a problem for it to solve.

   A column needs none of this: its entries are already as wide as the column. */

.footer .button {
    /* A floor rather than a width. Even was the whole point of the number and
       even is what it still is for every word that fits; what it cannot be any
       more is a promise, because a word that does not fit was being painted
       outside its own frame and over the button beside it. Russian is wider
       than English at the same size, the text setting adds a third on top, and
       "По умолчанию" wanted two hundred pixels of a hundred and sixty. */
    min-width: 160px;

    margin-left: var(--space-s);

    -unity-text-align: middle-center;
}


